00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.550 align:start position:0%
 
what's up y'all I'm here to talk about  the Callisto protocol after playing  about 90 minutes of the game during a  preview event  a lot of what I played was expected the  setting and enemy designs are certainly  unnerving but in a way I've seen before  quite literally a week prior when John  Luke and I were previewing the Dead  Space remake but the Callisto protocol  stands out and what it does differently  from its spiritual successor that it  injects a compelling melee combat system  into a survival horror game  many survival horror games keep their  monsters more than an arm's length away  allowing you to keep them at Bay with a  handgun plasma cutter camera or some  other manner of clicking and shooting  the Callisto protocol breaks that mold  amping its tension by encouraging you to  aggressively deal with whatever gross  monster you're fighting and that makes  for some fun combat developed by  Striking Distance Studios the Callisto  protocol sees you play as Jacob Lee  who's being kept in Black Iron a prison  located upon Callisto one of Jupiter's  moons Jacob quickly finds himself  needing to escape as a deadly infection  sweeps black Iron transforming the  former human inhabitants into monstrous  mutations referred to as biophages so  far Jacob seems to match the energy of  the male survival horror protagonists  we've seen in recent years often  speaking to himself when figuring out  where to go for his next objective or  cursing out the monsters he's going up  against following a specially harrowing  encounter he certainly cuts the tension  a tad more than I would like but the  Callisto protocol's atmosphere is so  unsettling feeling that it claws its way  back under your skin quickly enough  instead of cinematic cut scenes and  regular conversations between Jacob and  the other human survivors the Callisto  protocol seems to build most of its  story with audio logs some of which  detail truly unsettling events these are  all optional so you can miss out on a  lot of lore if you just speed through  the events of the story more so than the  story what struck me most about the  Callisto protocol is its combat lots of  survival horror protagonists are either  trained in the use of firearms or  everyday people trying to make do with  makeshift weapons that can keep the  monsters at Bay regardless there's a  sense of precision to combat in these  games whether it means pulling off the  perfect headshot or lining up a limb to  sever similarly the clista protocol  prioritizes Precision but by putting  that emphasis on melee combat you  suddenly need to pull off exact  movements in the heat of a bra instead  of from a distance it's not unlike the  sensation of playing a game like for  Honor albeit in a Sci-Fi setting you're  also fighting some truly gross looking  monsters and a lot of of ways the  biophages are very similar to dead space  as Necromorphs both enemies are highly  mutated creatures that stretch and  contort the human form into something  while monstrous manages to still be  terrifyingly recognizable the cluster  protocol manages to break free from dead  space's Shadow by changing how you deal  with this threat forcing you to act with  Precision within lunging range which is  a whole different kind of trepidation to  deal with this does lessen the Fear  Factor of these creatures a bit though a  zombie or Necromorph or ghostly Spirit  that's being kept at a distance can be  viewed in all its ghastly Glory it's far  harder when the enemy is right in your  face I never really had the chance to be  horrified at the enemy design in the  Callisto protocol given how in your face  the biophages are though their  aggressive nature certainly makes for  some tense combat encounters  mechanically the clista protocol uses a  combat system composed of attacks and  Dodges that are meant to encourage you  to be hyper aggressive against whatever  you're fighting Jacob also comes  equipped with a firearm but ammo is  limited and the gun doesn't do nearly as  much damage as your melee attacks safer  when an enemy is momentarily stunned  when this occurs you have a brief moment  to quickly pull out your gun and fire at  the highlighted weak spot doing so deals  a tremendous amount of damage to  whatever you're fighting even  potentially carving off the limbs or  head of your foe this can alter your  situation by quite a bit a biophage  without legs will have to slowly crawl  its way towards you for example there  was one moment during my preview that I  hope is an indication that we'll see  more strategic dismemberment in the full  release when I can only imagine is a  cheeky reference back to Dead Space  there is a moment in the Callisto  protocol where you find a message  Written in Blood on the wall that says  shoot the tentacles this event precedes  encountering biophages with  tentacle-line stomachs that will mutate  into more powerful forms once weakened  to deal with them you have to damage  them enough to start mutating which  begins with their stomach tentacles  erupting from their body in this moment  you have precious time to shoot and  destroy the tentacles stopping the  transformation it adds a bit more  strategy to the combat since you want to  keep a little distance from the  biophages that can mutate just so you  can actually see when the process is  starting if they get too close the  animation is oftentimes lost in the  flurry of lens  initially I approached the Callisto  protocol much like dead space Resident  Evil or The Evil Within carefully  weighing the situation and warily  approaching each fight trying to take  out one enemy at a time and then pulling  back to reassess that saw me die a lot  because you can't instantly heal in the  Callisto protocol to heal Jacob crouches  takes out a syringe and slowly injects  himself you're a Sitting Duck for a good  while in a game where the enemies might  Lumber towards you even within a group a  healing system like this would be no  problem but enemies in the Callisto  protocol hit hardest when they're in  your face and do all they can to close  the distance on you quickly with a very  little room to breathe I quickly found  that it is best to heal after a fight  has ended not during that means you also  have to hit hard and fast against every  enemy in the group ending the battle as  quickly as you can in this way the  cluster protocol encourages you to turn  the tables on your foes you you have to  be the monster in the room pursuing the  biophages with murderous abandon you  want to be overly Reckless of course as  that'll see you end up dead in no time  but I found far more success in the  Callisto protocol when I threw caution  to the wind and risked a few hits to  land half a dozen of my own instead of  using the telekinetic grip device to  grab objects and throw them at enemies I  most often used to pull enemies towards  me and land an early strike during an  especially harrowing moment in a very  narrow hallway featuring a murderous  parade of enemies I only succeeded when  I decided to push forward through the  deadly Onslaught and meet the biophages  head on quickly bashing their first few  into the walls to give me the precious  seconds necessary to dodge the blows of  those who came right after and then  start fighting them all at once sending  each in quick succession playing it safe  and being careful only gave the enemies  enough time to Bunch together and  overwhelm me but being aggressive helped  turn the tables on them the combat isn't  all that scary the violence in the game  is brutal and super gross but in a way  that feels geared towards producing  shock as opposed to genuine fear the  Callisto protocol is certainly a  survival experience with scarce  resources and hard-hitting enemies  always encouraging you to forage for  ammo or a health pack but the section I  played lacked the unnerving sense of  discomfort I love in my favorite  survival horror games I never really  felt like Jacob was ever on the back  foot the dude is an exceptionally  capable brawler and once I got used to  the combat I was holding my own against  the biophages I never felt like I was a  dominant Force but I wasn't just some  guy totally out of his League either  this isn't necessarily a bad thing as I  really enjoyed what I played of the  Callisto protocols combat I just want to  warn folks that if you're expecting the  Callisto protocol to be this terrifying  horror experience that's not the vibe I  got from the snippet of the game I  played the setting is spooky the  monsters are gross but this game doesn't  seem to match the truly horrific tone of  its initial announcement trailer granted  almost the entire preview was combat  Gauntlet after combat Gauntlet I didn't  have much chance to see elements of the  cluster protocol Story play out or  whether or not the game featured much in  the way of puzzles or horrifying  Mysteries to solve and most notably the  section I play didn't feature  opportunities to see how the game's  stealth moments worked if they're there  these elements could be where a lot of  the cluster protocol's Fear Factor come  from there was one moment during the  session where I was able to sneak around  as I slowly Crouch walked past a robotic  guard and that was nerve-wracking but  the section came and went in a Flash  hardly enough time to get a good idea as  to how the Callisto Protocol Stealth  sections play and feel more broadly but  is just enough time to offer an  intriguing Glimpse at what could be  quite scary something that I did notice  during that small stealth section that  gives me pause is that you have very  little control over the camera in the  clista protocol making it very difficult  to see anything over Jacob's left  shoulder which is where his character  model takes up the most screen real  estate you really only have peripheral  vision on your right personally I'm  worried that this could result in  frustrating death death specifically  during the stealth segments as a big  part of stealth games is the ability to  look around and plan I imagine that's  much harder to do in a game like the  Callisto protocol given the limits of  the camera thankfully the camera didn't  create any huge problems for me during  the combat but I think this restriction  will be something to look out for once  the full game launches and we get a  chance to play through the stealth  sections especially if there happen to  be scenarios where if you're not careful  you just die  I see what's going on  the terrifying possibility of instacle  deaths aside I came away from the  Callisto protocol feeling tense from the  pressure of the enclosed setting and  more than a little unnerved by The  Game's enemy design but I wasn't scared  or terrified of the situation I played  through Jacob is a beast once you get  the hang of his Dodges and melee strikes  and pulling foes into your Mighty swing  makes quick enough work of them to make  you feel at least on par with these  monsters at least in this one level the  Calisto protocol seems to be a survival  game that prioritizes the challenge of  its action-focused combat aiming to  create horror and the prospect that you  have to get into an enemy's Grill to do  the most damage and suffer disgusting  death animation if you mess up once  you've grasped how combat Works however  you seem to be one of the scariest  monsters in the prison obviously since  it's just a preview and I only got to  play through a presumably small section  of the game it's way too early to make a  definitive call on the kalista protocol  a lot of what could make the game more  horrifying and scary might be tucked  away in parts of the Callisto protocol  I've yet to see we won't know until the  game comes out but thankfully we don't  have long to wait because the Calista  protocol Launches on December 2nd for  more on the Callisto protocol you can  read through my written preview of The  Game on gamespot.com which includes  quotes and insight from my interview  with Striking Distance Studios CEO Glenn  Schofield and if you're in the mood for  more survival horror I'd recommend  watching your video previews of the Dead  Space remake and the Resident Evil 4  remake  [Music]

